        "h2": "The Base: Price Per Linear Foot",
        "content": "Almost every sound gutter pricing model starts with linear footage, because footage tracks effort better than any other single number. Typical residential rates run roughly a dollar to a dollar fifty per linear foot for single-story work, so a 160-foot ranch lands around 160 to 240 before adders, with markets varying by region and season. Footage is also easy to defend to a customer, your house has about 180 feet of gutter sounds like measurement, not guesswork, and easy to capture once per property from a curb pace-off or a satellite trace. Resist the temptation to quote flat by house type; two colonials can differ by 80 feet of gutter, and flat pricing means one of those customers is subsidizing the other. The per-foot base also scales cleanly into commercial work, where runs are long, and into guard installs later, which price per foot as well. Set the number so that your realistic stops-per-day at average footage clears your revenue target per truck, then let the surcharges handle everything that makes a house harder than average."
